WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.255 --> 00:00:01.920
SPEAKER: Observability
is increasingly

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.370
required of our systems as we
work to improve reliability.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.250
Metrics represent the health
of your system over time

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.040
and are a crucial
component of observability.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.010
And while services running in
GCP benefit from out-of-the-box

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.820
platform instrumentation, it's
often necessary to add more.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.090
Today, we'll take a look at
how to do that with OpenCensus

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.260
and Stackdriver.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.900
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.369
Last time, we looked at how
our microservices can benefit

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.650
from a service mesh that
exports monitoring, logging,

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.620
and tracing data to Stackdriver.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.040
Now, let's add our own
telemetry with custom metrics.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.080
When considering adding
metric instrumentation

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.000
to your applications,
you generally

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.770
have three options
to choose from.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.350
Your first option is to use
the Stackdriver Monitoring

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:55.390
API to send metrics and data
points to Stackdriver directly.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.670
This is what the Stackdriver
monitoring agent does.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.410
However, you should
know that this API

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.880
is low level and proprietary.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.053
And at this point, we rarely
recommend using it directly.

00:01:04.053 --> 00:01:05.470
Let's look at some
more extensible

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.620
and easier-to-use options.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.040
Your second option
is to use Prometheus.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.650
It's an open source
monitoring solution.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.210
Because using
Prometheus involves

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.030
more than just
instrument and your code,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.680
we'll come back to this
in our next episode.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.050
Your third option and
the one we recommend

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.330
for creating your own custom
metric instrumentation

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.065
is OpenCensus.

00:01:26.065 --> 00:01:27.690
It's a single
distribution of libraries

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.950
that collect metrics
and distributed traces

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.028
from your services.

00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:33.570
Let's look at how
to use it to create

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.550
custom metrics and Stackdriver.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.710
We'll use Node.js
for this example.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:41.040
First, we need to install
the two required packages--

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.413
OpenCensus itself and
the Stackdriver Explorer.

00:01:44.413 --> 00:01:46.080
We'll do that by
installing the packages

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.360
with NPM install
at the command line

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.760
or include them as part
of the Docker file.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.398
Next, we'll include these
packages in our code.

00:01:53.398 --> 00:01:55.440
This needs to be done at
the very top of the file

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.180
before any other code.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.720
We are now ready to
start creating metrics.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.400
But let's review the basic
concepts of the OpenCensus data

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.600
model first.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.550
To start, we'll need a view.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.620
It'll describe how to
collect and aggregate

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.389
individual measurements.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.430
Views can be thought of
as the time series we'll

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.920
be adding points to later.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.350
Next, we'll use measures
to describe how the data

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.860
points will be recorded.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.810
We'll also specify
an aggregation,

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.450
which is a function
to summarize the data.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.330
Finally, we'll
create measurements.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.587
These will be our
actual data points.

00:02:26.587 --> 00:02:28.920
We'll need to use the view
we created to aggregate them.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.470
Otherwise, it'll get
dropped on ingest.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.060
Now that we've covered
the data model,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.435
let's walk through
the code we'll

00:02:34.435 --> 00:02:37.260
need to write to actually
create and send metrics.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.270
We'll put a link to the
repo and my actual code

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.960
and a detailed solution paper
in the video description.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.270
First, we'll define
our export interval

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.335
and create the measure.

00:02:46.335 --> 00:02:48.210
This is where we define
the metric that we'll

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.680
be sending the Stackdriver.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.430
The key things here
are the fact that it's

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.120
going to be an Int64 value and
the name of the description

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.530
of the metric.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.170
Next, we'll use that
definition to actually register

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.113
the metric with Stackdriver.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.530
We're calling to
create new method

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.900
to create the view and then
the register view method

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.520
to actually register it.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.790
Note that I'm using the
last value aggregation type.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.350
This means that
only the last value

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.290
that was captured during the
export interval will be sent.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.610
You can also use distribution
count and some aggregations.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.370
After that, we'll register
the Stackdriver exporter.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.870
This is the mechanism
that will send

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.540
the metrics to the Stackdriver
monitoring backend.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.780
Finally, we are ready to
create actual data points.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.500
We do that by referring to
the measure we already created

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.980
and specifying a value.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.720
We are finally ready
to run our code

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.040
and send the metric
data to the Stackdriver.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.410
And there it is.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.330
I can see the metric
in Metrics Explorer.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.783
It has the name I assigned it.

00:03:40.783 --> 00:03:42.450
And it's showing the
last value that was

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.647
generated when I ran the code.

00:03:44.647 --> 00:03:46.980
This can be an easy way to
expose additional information

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.147
about the performance and
health of your application

00:03:49.147 --> 00:03:51.720
to Stackdriver
thanks to OpenCensus.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.663
Thank you for joining
me today and come back

00:03:53.663 --> 00:03:55.830
next time when we look at
another approach to custom

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.540
metrics with Prometheus.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:58.770
That's it for this episode.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.240
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.230
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

